## Context

Before this tool, an agent that needed to reach the brain mid-task had to
already know which backend was wired and which MCP tools to call against
it. That knowledge was scattered across the `/aide:brain` command prose,
agent definitions, and skill descriptions, every one of them naming
`mcp__obsidian__*` tools as if Obsidian were the only possible backend.
Adding a second backend (Notion, GitHub Wiki, hosted PKB, anything else)
would have meant editing every one of those surfaces in lockstep — a
fan-out the surface was never built to carry, and a guarantee that some
surface would lag and tell agents to call tools that don't exist.

The settled architectural insight from `.aide/handoff.aide` is that the
abstraction layer already exists, just one step deeper than the agent
sees. `provisionBrain` seeds a `CLAUDE.md` at the brain's root that owns
folder structure, search semantics, and write rules. Once an agent reads
that file, the brain takes over — no further server help needed. So the
only piece of backend knowledge that has to live on the server side is
**how to reach that entry-point**: which MCP key identifies the wired
backend, which read tool the agent should call, and what path inside the
backend the seeded `CLAUDE.md` lives at. Everything past the entry-point
is the brain's job; everything up to it is `aide_brain`'s.

The boundary that makes this clean is "until the agent is reading the
seeded `CLAUDE.md`, the server owns navigation; from that file forward,
the brain owns it." The registry of backends only carries entry-point
reach prose. It does not encode search, read, write, or any other
operation per backend — those grow inside the brain's own seeded file,
without ever touching this server's surface. Adding a backend is one
registry entry.

This work does not bolt onto `aide_info` because the two tools serve
different cadences. `aide_info` is the boot probe: it fires on every
`/aide` invocation as a precondition gate, and its `brain.status`
tagged union is the same shape this tool reuses. Runtime brain reach,
by contrast, is needed only when an agent actually reaches for the
brain mid-task — which is the minority of invocations. Bundling
runtime instructions into the boot probe would bloat every `/aide`
boot with prose that gets thrown away. Splitting them into a separate
on-demand tool keeps the boot lean and lets the runtime tool grow its
own response without coupling to boot semantics.

## Strategy

**Backend detection mirrors `buildBrainState` exactly — same MCP-key
scan, same tagged-union vocabulary.** The tool reads the host's
framework-specific MCP config the same way `aide_info` does (via
`detectFramework` to resolve the config path), then probes a single
known key per backend (today `"obsidian"`). The status field carries
`ok | no-mcp-entry | invalid-path` — identical to `aide_info.brain.status`.
Reusing the existing detection pattern means the tool inherits all the
edge cases `buildBrainState` already handles (missing config, malformed
JSON, missing entry, empty path, broken path) without re-deriving them.
Reusing the vocabulary means an agent that already saw `brain.status`
at boot does not have to translate between two boundary surfaces. This
traces directly to the desired outcome that status vocabulary mirrors
`aide_info` exactly.

**`instructions` is server-assembled, ready-to-execute prose — never
structured fields.** When a backend is wired, the server composes a
multi-sentence prose block per backend that names the specific MCP read
tool the agent should call, the path to the brain's seeded entry-point
file, and a closing sentence telling the agent the brain takes over from
there. The agent receives prose it can act on directly; it never has to
read a structured contract and assemble its own usage instructions. This
is the load-bearing decision the handoff settled: server owns backend
knowledge, agent stays simple, adding a backend is one prose template
in the registry rather than a contract change every consumer has to
absorb. This traces to the desired outcome that the agent gets enough
in one response to navigate without further server calls, and to the
undesired outcome that forbids structured fields the agent has to
assemble.

**The scope of `instructions` stops at the entry-point — full stop.**
The prose tells the agent how to reach the brain's seeded `CLAUDE.md`
at the vault root. It does not enumerate folder structure, search
semantics, write rules, or any operation past that file — those live
inside the seeded `CLAUDE.md` itself, which the agent is one read away
from. This is the load-bearing decision from the handoff: the registry
stays narrow because the brain's own navigation rules grow inside the
brain, never inside this server. Encoding "how to search" or "how to
write" per backend in the registry would explode the per-backend
surface, drift against the seeded file the moment the user customized
their vault, and replicate work the brain's `CLAUDE.md` already does.
This traces to the undesired outcome that forbids encoding brain
operations in the driver registry and the undesired outcome that
forbids duplicating what the seeded `CLAUDE.md` owns.

**The backend driver registry lives in its own module, not inside this
tool.** The handoff names a likely location at `src/service/brainBackends/`.
This tool consumes the registry — it dispatches on the detected MCP key
and asks the registry for the matching backend's reach prose. The
registry owns the per-backend data; the tool owns the dispatch. This
separation is what makes "adding a backend = one registry entry" true:
the tool's logic does not need to learn about the new backend, only the
registry does.

**When no backend is wired, `instructions` carries remediation prose,
never empty.** A `no-mcp-entry` response tells the agent how to wire
the brain — concretely, "run `/aide:brain config` to wire the brain"
— so the agent surfaces a wiring prompt to the user instead of
silently proceeding without the brain. An `ok` response with empty
`instructions` is a forbidden state: it tells the agent the brain is
reachable but gives it no way to reach it, which is the silent-failure
mode the spec explicitly bans. This traces to the desired outcome that
remediation prose is never empty and the undesired outcome that forbids
silent success.

**`backend` carries the wired identifier when status is `ok`, and is
`null` otherwise.** The field is a structured signal of which branch
fired — programmatic consumers like the `/aide:brain` Mode 2 shim can
branch on `backend === null` without parsing prose. This is the
parallel-but-not-redundant relationship the spec calls for: the
structured signal answers "which branch?", the prose answers "what
should the agent do?". Conflating the two by encoding the backend
identifier inside the prose only would force every consumer to parse
strings; conflating them by repeating the same English statement in
both fields would be redundant. Keeping them parallel — one structured,
one executable — is what lets the shim reduce to "call `aide_brain`,
follow the `instructions` field." This traces to the desired outcome
that pins this exact field semantics.

**The tool integrates as a peer to `aide_info` in the MCP server's
tool registration, not as a child of it.** It is invoked on demand by
agents reaching for the brain mid-task, never fired by the orchestrator
on `/aide` boot. `aide_info` keeps its precondition-gate role; this
tool keeps its runtime-reach role; the two never call each other. This
traces to the undesired outcome that forbids being called from
`aide_info` or fired on every boot.

**The tool never proxies, wraps, or translates calls to the backend's
native MCP tools.** Its sole output is prose telling the agent what
those native tools are and how to use them. The agent then calls those
native tools directly. Putting a CRUD-style proxy layer here would
defeat the entire architectural point: backends bring their own MCP
tools, and the seeded `CLAUDE.md` already orchestrates them. A proxy
would add a translation surface that has to be maintained per backend
on top of the seeded file that already governs the same operations.
This traces to the undesired outcome that forbids wrapping or
translating native backend calls.

## Good examples

A project with the obsidian backend wired and the vault resolving on
disk:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": "obsidian",
  "instructions": "Use mcp__obsidian__read_note to read CLAUDE.md at the vault root. That file is the brain's navigation contract — folder structure, search protocol, decision protocol, and where-to-find-things. Once you have read it, follow the rules it defines for the rest of your task; the brain takes over from there and this server has no further role in your navigation."
}
```

The agent acts on the prose directly: it calls `mcp__obsidian__read_note`
on `CLAUDE.md`, reads the seeded navigation contract, and proceeds.
The shim that called `aide_brain` reduces to "follow the `instructions`
field" — no Obsidian-specific prose anywhere in the shim, and no
backend awareness leaked into the agent definitions.

A project that has never run `/aide:brain config` — no obsidian entry
in the MCP config:

```json
{
  "status": "no-mcp-entry",
  "backend": null,
  "instructions": "No brain backend is wired in this project. Surface this to the user and recommend running /aide:brain config to wire the brain. Do not proceed with this task as if the brain were available — the user must wire it before brain-dependent work continues."
}
```

The agent surfaces a wiring prompt to the user and stops. `backend` is
`null` so the shim can branch programmatically without parsing prose.
`instructions` is non-empty and carries the remediation directly.

A project where the obsidian entry exists but the configured vault path
no longer resolves on disk:

```json
{
  "status": "invalid-path",
  "backend": null,
  "instructions": "An obsidian backend is configured but its vault path does not resolve on disk. Surface this to the user and recommend running /aide:brain config to repoint the vault at its current location. Do not proceed with this task as if the brain were available — the path must be corrected before brain-dependent work continues."
}
```

`backend` is `null` because the brain is not currently reachable —
status is the structured signal the agent acts on, and the prose names
the precise remediation. The status distinction between `no-mcp-entry`
and `invalid-path` lets the prose match the user's actual state instead
of a generic "brain missing" message.

## Bad examples

A response that names obsidian-specific tools when a hypothetical
non-obsidian backend is wired:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": "notion",
  "instructions": "Use mcp__obsidian__read_note to read CLAUDE.md at the vault root..."
}
```

Violates the desired outcome that the agent gets ready-to-execute
prose for whatever backend is wired. The agent will call a tool that
either doesn't exist in this project or operates on the wrong backend,
and fails immediately. The registry's per-backend prose template is
the single mechanism that prevents this — a hardcoded obsidian path
through the dispatcher would silently produce this output for every
non-obsidian backend.

A bare-reference response that gives the agent a pointer instead of
usage prose:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": "obsidian",
  "instructions": "obsidian, vault root CLAUDE.md"
}
```

Violates the undesired outcome that bans bare references. The agent has
to interpret what to do with "obsidian, vault root CLAUDE.md" — call
which tool? Read which way? The point of `instructions` is to be
ready-to-execute prose composed by the server, not a thin pointer the
agent has to assemble into a tool call.

A silent-success response with `ok` status but empty `instructions`:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": null,
  "instructions": ""
}
```

Violates the undesired outcome that forbids silent success. The agent
sees `status: "ok"`, assumes the brain is reachable, and proceeds with
no idea how to reach it — either failing later when it tries to read
something, or just skipping the brain entirely without telling the user.
`instructions` must always be non-empty; an `ok` status with no prose
is structurally inconsistent.

A response whose `instructions` duplicates what the seeded `CLAUDE.md`
already owns:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": "obsidian",
  "instructions": "Use mcp__obsidian__read_note to read CLAUDE.md at the vault root. Then follow the wikilink crawling protocol: hub notes are navigation, content notes are depth 0, never re-read, stay in scope. Coding conventions go through the study-playbook skill; everything else uses mcp__obsidian__search_notes scoped to the right directory. Research lives under research/, retros under process/retro/, the playbook under coding-playbook/..."
}
```

Violates the undesired outcome that forbids duplicating what the
seeded `CLAUDE.md` owns. Folder structure, crawling protocol, and
where-to-find-things are the brain's own navigation rules, written
into the seeded `CLAUDE.md` at provision time. Re-stating them in
`instructions` creates two sources of truth that drift the moment the
user customizes their vault, and bloats every brain reach with
information the agent is one read away from anyway.

A CRUD-style response that wraps the backend's native MCP tools:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": "obsidian",
  "operations": {
    "read": "aide_brain_read",
    "search": "aide_brain_search",
    "write": "aide_brain_write"
  }
}
```

Violates the undesired outcome that forbids wrapping or translating
native backend calls. The agent now has to learn a translation layer
maintained per backend on top of the seeded `CLAUDE.md` that already
orchestrates the same operations. Every backend has to implement
matching wrappers for every operation the brain might use, and the
seeded file's rules and the wrapper's rules drift the moment they
disagree. The tool's contract is "tell the agent how to use the
backend's native tools," not "intermediate every call."

## References

- `.aide/handoff.aide` -- settled architectural decisions: the brain
  owns navigation past the entry-point, `instructions` is pre-formatted
  prose not structured fields, backend detection mirrors
  `buildBrainState`, `aide_info` stays a fast boot probe while runtime
  reach lives in this new tool. Drove every Strategy decision in this
  spec.
- `src/tools/info/.aide` -- closest sibling spec; provided the tagged-
  union vocabulary (`ok | no-mcp-entry | invalid-path`) this tool
  reuses verbatim and the structured-state-not-prose contract that
  shapes the `backend === null` parallel-not-redundant decision.
- `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/index.ts` -- canonical detection
  implementation; established the single-key MCP probe (`"obsidian"`),
  the `detectFramework`-then-read-MCP-config flow, and every edge case
  (missing config, malformed JSON, broken args, empty vault path)
  this tool inherits by reusing the same detection helper.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts` -- defines what gets
  seeded into the brain at install time; confirms the entry-point
  file is `CLAUDE.md` at the vault root, that the seeded file owns
  folder structure / search protocol / decision protocol, and that
  `mcp__obsidian__read_note` and `mcp__obsidian__search_notes` are
  the obsidian-side tools the entry-point reach prose names.
- `src/tools/discover/.aide` -- sibling tool spec; reference for the
  shape and depth of "decision-form" Strategy paragraphs and the
  pattern of citing parent specs inline.
- `src/tools/upgrade/.aide` -- sibling tool spec; reference for an
  agent-driven structured-response tool whose contract terminates at
  "agent acts on the structured field directly," same posture this
  tool takes with `instructions`.
- `src/.aide` -- parent intent and submodule strategy; established
  the no-doctrine-as-string-literals invariant (the brain's seeded
  `CLAUDE.md` is the single source of truth for navigation, not
  duplicated in this tool's prose) and the one-tool-per-phase rule
  that justifies splitting runtime brain reach out of `aide_info`.
- `.claude/commands/aide/brain.md` -- current Mode 2 command; the
  Obsidian-specific prose this tool's `instructions` field replaces,
  and the shim that will collapse to "call `aide_brain`, follow
  `instructions`" once this tool ships.
